  "response": "Resolving the matter required characters that a mere infolding of host membrane could not plausibly carry. Margit Nass and Sylvan Nass reported DNA inside mitochondria in 1963 using electron microscopy, and their micrographs show that mitochondria retain a genome of their own that is distinct from the nuclear genome. On this point the possibilities are strict and mutually exclusive: either mitochondria retain such a separate genome of their own, or mitochondria contain no DNA of their own, and exactly one of these two situations can obtain.\n\nA further line of work addressed the biochemistry of the organelle rather than its appearance in section, using the tools of pharmacology and lipid analysis. At least one of the following is the case, and quite possibly both at once: mitochondrial ribosomes are inhibited by the antibacterial antibiotic chloramphenicol, and the mitochondrial inner membrane contains cardiolipin, a lipid characteristic of bacterial membranes.\n\nThe decisive evidence came from the comparison of sequences rather than of structures. Carl Woese established small-subunit ribosomal RNA sequence comparison as a method for reconstructing deep phylogeny, and that methodological advance led directly to the placement of the mitochondrial lineage inside the alphaproteobacteria by ribosomal RNA phylogenies. A ribosomal placement among the alphaproteobacteria and a placement among the cyanobacteria cannot both be true of one and the same organelle, and phylogenetic analyses of mitochondrial genes place the organelle within the cyanobacteria. The alphaproteobacterial affiliation indicates that mitochondria arose from a free-living bacterium that was engulfed by an ancestral host cell; in other words, the mitochondrial lineage descends from a prokaryotic symbiont that was taken up by an ancestral cell.\n\nThe manner in which the controversy closed is instructive about method in evolutionary cell biology. Ultrastructural appearance alone could not discriminate between the competing accounts of organelle origin, since an infolded membrane compartment and an engulfed cell may look much alike in thin section, and arguments from morphology were accordingly inconclusive for decades. What discriminated in the end was heritable information: nucleic acid read as sequence and interpreted as a branching genealogy. Once comparative sequence analysis supplied a determinate branching order with identifiable prokaryotic relatives, the question ceased to be a matter of competing interpretations of images and became one admitting a testable answer.\n\nThe question was contested from the moment the modern endosymbiotic proposal appeared in print. Lynn Margulis published the paper \"On the Origin of Mitosing Cells\" in 1967, at a time when the ultrastructure of eukaryotic organelles was far better characterized than their ancestry. Margulis argued that mitochondria descended from free-living aerobic bacteria taken up by a host cell, and that argument cannot be squared with the proposal, set out in Margulis's 1967 paper, that mitochondria arose by invagination of the host plasma membrane; one origin excludes the other, so the two cannot both hold of the same organelle. By contrast, the autogenous hypothesis held that mitochondria formed by the infolding of the host cell's own membranes.",
